 with Seattle's rotated grid (true north up): Paul Thiry's low, nearly windowless galleries (1952) as extended and remade by Olson Sundberg (1997) - pale walls, long skylight monitors over the galleries, the rotunda lobby lit from a clerestory, the long reflecting pool and the arcade of plain columns along the entrance ramp on Terry Avenue, the glazed café and courtyard at the south corner; street trees. From the museum's self-guided tour, HistoryLink and published descriptions; guessed: the block outline, the heights (about 7 m; the rotunda 12 m), the monitors, the courtyard's place, and the grid's angle. No photographs were available."}